// Zed project configuration for Maverick.
//
// Deliberately contains NO settings. rust-analyzer's defaults already describe
// this repository's real Cargo workspace, and the rule for this project is that
// `cargo`/`rustc` decide whether the code compiles — the editor must not
// deviate from that. Anything added here would be a second source of truth.
//
// What rust-analyzer does by default (verified with Zed 1.20.2 + the distro
// rust-analyzer, including rust-analyzer's own flycheck output under
// `target/flycheck0/`):
//
//   * Workspace — `Cargo.toml` at the repository root *is* the Cargo workspace
//     root (the root `maverick` package plus the members it declares).
//     rust-analyzer resolves packages through Cargo, so every member is
//     analysed. No `linkedProjects` needed.
//
//   * Features — do NOT enable everything (`cargo.allFeatures`, or
//     `cargo.features: ["all"]`): `input-trace` and `window-trace` are
//     diagnostic-only builds, so that would analyse a configuration nobody
//     develops against. CI agrees —
//     `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs plain `cargo clippy --workspace
//     --all-targets` and `cargo test --workspace`, without `--all-features`.
//
//   * Check command — rust-analyzer's default is `cargo check --workspace
//     --all-targets` with default features, so cargo stays the authority.
//     `--all-targets` is intentional: it also covers `#[cfg(test)]` code, like
//     CI does. `cargo check --workspace` alone is a subset of that, so a
//     diagnostic in test code that plain `cargo check` does not print is real,
//     not a false positive.
//
//   * Toolchain — this project uses the distro rustc/cargo/rust-analyzer, not
//     rustup. rust-analyzer also needs the *sources* of the standard library;
//     on Arch that is the `rust-src` package. Without it, rust-analyzer reports
//       "Workspace .../Cargo.toml has sysroot errors:
//        can't load standard library from sysroot"
//     and Zed shows bogus `std::`/`core` errors on every file while
//     `cargo check` stays clean (rustc ships the compiled `std`, so cargo is
//     unaffected). That mismatch is a missing component, not a code problem.
//
//   * Zed prefers the system `rust-analyzer` over its own downloaded copy
//     ("found user-installed language server for rust-analyzer"). Pinning a
//     binary is machine-specific, so if it is ever needed it belongs in the
//     user's global settings:
//       "lsp": { "rust-analyzer": { "binary": { "path": "/usr/bin/rust-analyzer" } } }
{}
